On Windows, look for a library named without a path in the directory of the module loading it.

SharedLibrary::loadWithPlatformPath (source/core/slang-platform.cpp) loads a library first with
LoadLibraryExW(name, nullptr, LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DEFAULT_DIRS) and then with LoadLibraryW(name).
Slang loads its downstream libraries this way by bare name, for example slang-glslang, which
carries spirv-opt (source/compiler-core/slang-glslang-compiler.cpp:592), and dxcompiler.
LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DEFAULT_DIRS includes LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DLL_LOAD_DIR, but that flag applies
only to a fully qualified name, so the directory of slang-compiler.dll is never searched, although
the comment above the call lists it first. An application that loads slang-compiler.dll from
anywhere but its executable's directory (a plugin directory, a package's runtimes\win-x64\native\,
an interpreter started elsewhere) therefore gets no spirv-opt and no dxc. On Linux and macOS the
same layout works, because the libraries carry $ORIGIN and @loader_path rpaths.

This adds a third attempt for a name without a path, after the two existing ones fail: the
directory of the module this code is linked into, found with GetModuleHandleExW and
GetModuleFileNameW, loaded with a full path. A library either existing attempt finds is found
as before. The behaviour depends on how the process is launched, so it is not covered by a unit
test; the measurement below is a process started from an unrelated directory.

Measured on v2026.21 (win-x64): `dotnet App.dll` run from an empty directory, with slang-compiler.dll
in the application's directory: "Slang cannot load spirv-opt, which lives in the 'slang-glslang'
shared library", SLANG_E_NOT_FOUND.
diff --git a/source/core/slang-platform.cpp b/source/core/slang-platform.cpp
index f4e29e885..e767e104b 100644
--- a/source/core/slang-platform.cpp
+++ b/source/core/slang-platform.cpp
@@ -200,6 +200,32 @@ SLANG_COMPILE_TIME_ASSERT(E_OUTOFMEMORY == SLANG_E_OUT_OF_MEMORY);
 
     if (!handle)
         handle = LoadLibraryW(wideFileName);
+
+    // LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DLL_LOAD_DIR applies only to a fully qualified name, so neither attempt
+    // above searches the directory of the module this code is in (slang-compiler.dll) for a bare
+    // name such as `slang-glslang`. Where neither found the library, try that directory, as the
+    // rpaths do on Linux and macOS.
+    if (!handle && platformFileNameStr.indexOf('\\') < 0 && platformFileNameStr.indexOf('/') < 0)
+    {
+        const DWORD notFound = GetLastError();
+        HMODULE self = nullptr;
+        wchar_t selfPath[MAX_PATH];
+        if (GetModuleHandleExW(
+                GET_MODULE_HANDLE_EX_FLAG_FROM_ADDRESS | GET_MODULE_HANDLE_EX_FLAG_UNCHANGED_REFCOUNT,
+                (LPCWSTR)&SharedLibrary::loadWithPlatformPath,
+                &self) &&
+            GetModuleFileNameW(self, selfPath, MAX_PATH) < MAX_PATH)
+        {
+            String directory = Path::getParentDirectory(String::fromWString(selfPath));
+            OSString widePath = Path::combine(directory, platformFileNameStr).toWString();
+            handle = LoadLibraryExW(
+                widePath,
+                nullptr,
+                LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DLL_LOAD_DIR | LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DEFAULT_DIRS);
+        }
+        if (!handle)
+            SetLastError(notFound);
+    }
     // If still not found, return an error.
     if (!handle)
     {
